    "lawCode": "Amd §99-w, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S2328A\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general municipal law, in relation to classifying\nmilitary monuments and memorials as parkland\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo provide additional statutory protection for veterans' memorials on\npublic property throughout New York State.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 2 of section 99-w of the General Municipal\nLaw to require that military monuments or memorials erected or\nconstructed pursuant to sections two hundred twenty-six of the County\nLaw, eighty-one of the Town Law, and seventy-two and seventy-a of the\nGeneral Municipal Law on property of a municipal corporation shall\nrequire authorization by an act of the Legislature prior to any change\nin status including development, leasing, transfer, sale or discontin-\nuance.\n\nSection 2 states that this law shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation will require an act of the Legislature in cases prior\nto a change in status of a military monument or memorial when such\nveterans' memorials are on publicly owned property. Currently, there are\nnot adequate protections in law to protect veterans' memorials.  This\nlegislation will protect veterans' memorials to ensure that the valuable\npurpose veterans memorials serve in our society will not be diminished.\n\nVeterans' monuments are deserving of additional protection. President\nFranklin Roosevelt held that view in 1933 when signed an executive order\nto bring historic battlefields and monuments under the control of the\nNational Park Service. Included was the Saratoga Battlefield in Stillwa-\nter.\n\nBy requiring an act of the Legislature for military monuments and memo-\nrials to be approved before changes in the status of these sites we will\nbe providing appropriate protection and oversight for these important\nsites.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20: S6677 (Mayer) referred to Rules (2019); referred to Local\nGovernment\n\n(2020) 2021-22: S242 (Mayer) referred to Local Government\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis law shall take effect immediately.\n",
